  [3mBuys:[0m almost no validation logic to own — the platform does it, and it's free for every future form. Rules sit next to
  the input they govern.
  [3mCosts:[0m rules become untestable without a real DOM, cross-field rules (confirm-password) have no home and need an
  escape hatch anyway, and the browser silently blocks submit on invalid fields unless you set [38;5;153mnovalidate[39m, which changes
  existing behavior.

  [1mC. Rules as code — one validate function per form[0m
  The module supplies only wiring: read [38;5;153mFormData[39m, call the form's [38;5;153m(values) => errors[39m function, route the result to the
  callback. No rule vocabulary at all.
  [3mBuys:[0m smallest possible thing; nothing to learn; maximum flexibility.
  [3mCosts:[0m it makes the [3mwiring[0m reusable, not the [3mvalidation[0m — which is what you asked for. Form two re-writes "is this
  empty" by hand.

  [1mI recommend A[0m, with the rule vocabulary capped at exactly what the login form needs today: [38;5;153mrequired[39m, and [38;5;153mminLength[39m
  only if you want it on the password. B is tempting and I nearly led with it, but it fails the specific thing you're
  buying — with no second form to validate the design against, being able to unit-test rules as plain values is most of
  the payoff, and B trades exactly that away. A can still borrow B's good idea later (reading [38;5;153mrequired[39m off the markup as
  a schema default) without re-deciding anything.

  One concrete blocker for any option that uses [38;5;153mFormData[39m: the inputs in [38;5;153mindex.html:9-10[39m have [38;5;153mid[39m but no [38;5;153mname[39m, and
  [38;5;153mFormData[39m only collects named fields. Adding [38;5;153mname="username"[39m / [38;5;153mname="password"[39m is part of the change.
[38;5;246m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[38;5;16m[48;5;153m ☐ Approach [39m[49m

[1m[38;5;231mWhich rule representation should we go with?[0m

[38;5;153m❯[39m [38;5;246m1.[39m [38;5;153mA: rules as data (Recommended)[39m
     [38;5;246mPer-form schema object built from small rule factories (required, minLength). Testable with no DOM; new form = one
[39m     [38;5;246mobject literal.[39m
  [38;5;246m2.[39m B: rules as markup
     [38;5;246mHTML constraint attributes + Constraint Validation API, normalized by a thin wrapper. Least logic to own; needs a [39m
     [38;5;246mDOM to test, awkward for cross-field rules.[39m
  [38;5;246m3.[39m C: one function per form
     [38;5;246mModule does wiring only; each form writes its own (values) => errors. Smallest change, but each form re-implements
[39m     [38;5;246mits rules.[39m
  [38;5;246m4. Type something.[39m
